[{"learnocaml_version":"2","kind":"exercise","title":"Safe Division","short_description":"Using `option`","stars":1,"identifier":"safe-div","authors":[["Max Kopinsky","max.kopinsky@mcgill.ca"],["Jacob Errington","jacob.errington@mcgill.ca"]]},{"id":"safe-div","prelude":"","template":"\n(* Implement safe_div which protects against division by zero. *)\nlet safe_div (n : int) (d : int) : int option =\n  failwith \"TODO\"\n\n(* Implement safe_div2, which does division twice using safe_div. *)\nlet safe_div2 (n : int) (d1 : int) (d2 : int) : int option =\n  failwith \"TODO\"\n","descr":[["","<h1 id=\"save-division-with-options\">Save Division With Options</h1>\n<p>In Monday's lecture, we saw the type</p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode ocaml\"><code class=\"sourceCode ocaml\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a><span class=\"kw\">type</span> &#39;a <span class=\"dt\">option</span> =</span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>  | <span class=\"dt\">None</span></span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"></a>  | <span class=\"dt\">Some</span> <span class=\"kw\">of</span> &#39;a</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>This type is actually in the standard library, so we don't need to\ndefine it in the prelude! Let's motivate using it with an example.</p>\n<p>Recall that division is undefined if the denominator is 0. For\nexample, 1/0 is undefined. What happens if you try <code>1 / 0</code> in\nthe REPL on LearnOCaml?</p>\n<h1 id=\"safe_div\">safe_div</h1>\n<p>To defend against division-by-zero, let's implement a function\n<code>safe_div</code>, which returns an <code>int option</code> instead\nof an <code>int</code>. When we would divide by zero, instead we \"fail\"\nby returning <code>None</code>. Otherwise, produce the correct answer\nwrapped up in a <code>Some</code> constructor.</p>\n<h1 id=\"chaining\">chaining</h1>\n<p>To practice with <em>using</em> options, instead of just producing\nthem, let's also write a function which does division twice.\n<code>safe_div2 n d1 d2</code> will compute <code>(n/d1)/d2</code>.</p>\n"]],"prepare":"tZ2RFBwA9bEiqvtESC8RXXVKXkt+C5tS359TPmx=","test":"","solution":"","max-score":40,"depend":null,"dependencies":[]},null]